d, do not reorder or reword the three existing entries.)\n\n## Why this entry and not another shape\n- `{limit:number}` matches `200` (and the word `no`, for a zero budget), so the one entry phrases every Scenario that bounds the reply delay, instead of hard-coding `200`.\n- `milliseconds` stays literal: the unit carries the meaning and there is no second unit in the catalogue to disambiguate against.\n- `block: then` matches the block the step sits in for E2E-001.\n- `terms: [mcp service, reply]` are spelled exactly as the glossary spells them; `reply` already states that a reply's delay is behaviour, so the meaning is closed without a new term.\n\n## Non-collision check the applier should confirm after editing\n- `the mcp service is running` → still matches only the `'the mcp service is running'` entry.\n- `the Operator sends \"ping\"` → still matches only the `'the {actor:role} sends \"{message}\"'` entry (`Operator` is a `roles[].name`).\n- `the service answers \"pong\"` → still matches only the `'the service answers \"{reply}\"'` entry; the new template requires the literal `within ` after `answers`, so it cannot match a quoted reply.\n- `the service answers within 200 milliseconds` → matches only the new entry; the quoted-reply template cannot match it because no `\"` follows `answers`.\n\nNo other step in any Scenario changes its matching entry.\n","questions":[]},"ttft_ms":1561,"type":"result","duration_ms":36659,"uuid":"22a13abe-9fe4-4313-bb77-2e1dc6dd2006","ttft_stream_ms":1015,"time_to_request_ms":41,"first_content_frame_ms":1015,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
